# Service conventions
These load when you read or edit a service's own parts: the API, the batch tasks, the business
operations, the database access and the migrations. `python-conventions.md` applies here too.

## Error Handling
- Raise `APIException.X(**context)` for structured errors — `APIException` is the enum of every wire
  code and the only place one is spelled; calling a member builds a fresh `APIError`, and the context
  keywords (ids, a reason) are what the refusal log line says beyond the code; never a shared
  instance or a free-form message
- OpenAPI error responses are derived from the raise statements (`{{ package_name }}/api/openapi_errors.py`),
  never declared on a route. The derivation follows bare-name calls between module-level functions
  under `{{ package_name }}.api`; a raise reached any other way — a method, `module.func()`, another package
  — reaches no operation, and a gate test fails naming the function

## Logging
- Use async log methods (`await log.ainfo()`, `await log.aerror()`) in async code, sync methods in sync code
- Batch tasks log nothing about their own run: `batch/main.py` logs start, finish and failure, and
  a task's returned dict of counts rides on the finish line

## Code Style
- Strategic abstractions at infrastructure (DB, ORM, queries), direct business logic
- Same code paths for batch and API work - no duplication
- A dependency a route runs only for its effect, such as a permission check whose value the route
  never reads, goes in the decorator's `dependencies=[Depends(...)]`, or the `APIRouter`'s for all
  its routes: FastAPI's idiom. As a parameter, it fails ruff's ARG
- Before adding a shared symbol, check its established owner: business operations in
  `{{ package_name }}/services.py`, technical helpers in `{{ package_name }}/utils/`, reusable database access in
  `{{ package_name }}/queries/`, API responses in `{{ package_name }}/api/v1/responses.py`, API-facing types in
  `{{ package_name }}/api/v1/types.py`, structured errors in `{{ package_name }}/api/errors.py`, and
  persistence/domain types in `{{ package_name }}/models/types.py`. Extend the owner instead of creating a
  parallel definition.

## Module Imports
- Runtime assembly (building apps/servers, wiring middleware) goes in `build_*()` factories
  that only the composition roots (`api/main.py`, `batch/main.py`) call
- Declarative registration stays module-level: `APIRouter()` + route decorators, Pydantic
  models, tool registries. The `config`/`services.py` singletons are the sanctioned exception

## Database
- ORM-first with SQLAlchemy, no raw SQL (except views)
- Bulk operations (`bulk_insert`, `bulk_upsert`) for performance-critical paths
- Load relationships explicitly: pass `resources=[...]` to the `CustomBase` helpers
  (`{{ package_name }}/database.py`), which load a collection with `selectinload` and a single object with
  `joinedload`. Declare every relationship `lazy="raise"`, so reading one you did not load fails
  and names it. SQLAlchemy's default `select` raises an opaque `MissingGreenlet` under async, and
  `noload` (deprecated in 2.1) silently returns `None`. A response that carries a relationship
  loads it on every path (for example, an `ITEM_RESPONSE_RESOURCES` list in `api/v1/core/items.py`)
- Guard `df.empty` before boolean masking or date arithmetic on a query result frame. Zero-row frames
  from `query_to_pandas_async` have object-typed columns, so a boolean mask can be read as column
  selection and return a zero-column frame, and date arithmetic can degrade the dtype and break a
  later merge. Cast around the symptom and the bug moves rather than goes
